# Bracha 1987, "Asynchronous Byzantine Agreement Protocols"
# Information and Computation 75, 130-143 (1987)
#
# Figure 1 -- the broadcast primitive, by process p, as a state machine.
#
# One run of these tables per event.  Two events: the arrival of a
# message of this broadcast, and a sweep of the caller's retry.  The
# state between runs is what earlier runs output and the caller
# applied -- "have echoed", "have sent ready", "have accepted",
# "abandoned" -- plus the counts, recorded on every arrival, as the
# figure's "(including messages received in step ...)" says, and two
# records per sender that BPR reads: recorded as holding the initial
# and recorded as having accepted, which the C records beside an
# (echo, v) or (ready, v) that carries HOLDING or ACCEPTED as it
# records the message itself.
#
# Three authors, three groups, composed by dtc:
#   the paper's rows   -- the figure's steps, verbatim; an output says
#                         the step's "wait until" is satisfied
#   the caller's rows  -- when a satisfied step is taken; abandon on
#                         the sweep
#   BPR's rows         -- what is re-sent on a sweep, and to whom:
#                         Bracha Phase Retry, the end-to-end argument
#                         (SRC84.txt) applied to the figure's sends.
#                         The replies an arrival returns to its sender
#                         are BPR's too, in bracha87Fig1Ready.dtc,
#                         compiled alone and run after this dispatch
#
# The chain is one-directional: paper -> caller -> BPR.  A caller row
# reads a paper output; a paper row of a later step reads the caller's
# "take" of the earlier step, because a step reads the state its
# predecessor LEFT (Section 2.1: "including those received at previous
# steps"; step 2's "I have echoed" is the echo step 1 just sent).
#
# Inputs (state, when the event is processed; v is the broadcast value):
#   "event"                          { arrival, sweep }
#   "received (initial, v) from p"   { yes, no }
#   "echo-count(v) > (n+t)/2"        { yes, no }
#   "ready-count(v) >= t+1"          { yes, no }
#   "ready-count(v) >= 2t+1"         { yes, no }
#   "have echoed"                    { yes, no }
#   "have sent ready"                { yes, no }
#   "have accepted"                  { yes, no }
#   "am initiator"                   { yes, no }   this process is p
#   "hold the caller's something from p" { yes, no }
#                                    the BESIDE: the caller's validation
#                                    of p's (initial, v).  Read at step
#                                    1's initial enabling alone: a
#                                    process echoes p's initial only with
#                                    that in hand.  The others' echoes
#                                    and readies are their word: the
#                                    first correct process to echo v did
#                                    so on p's initial, with its caller's
#                                    validation where its flag is set,
#                                    and the figure runs on them as
#                                    printed, as it does at steps 2 and
#                                    3.  A caller
#                                    with nothing to send beside says so
#                                    at Init, and this is yes
#   "processes holding (initial, v)" { all, not all }
#                                    the processes whose (echo, v) or
#                                    (ready, v) carried HOLDING, recorded
#   "sweeps since this process started"
#                                    { < abandon, >= abandon }
#                                    this process's sweeps since Init,
#                                    the current one counted -- Init is
#                                    where it starts: the initiator
#                                    takes step 0 and every other
#                                    process begins its wait at step 1
#                                    -- against the caller's abandon
#                                    count.  A process that runs this
#                                    figure inside a larger one counts
#                                    there and gives no count here
#   "abandoned"                      { yes, no }
#   "processes owed (ready, v)"      { none, some }
#                                    a process is owed it until recorded
#                                    as having accepted
#
# Outputs:
#   paper:   "send (echo, v)"  "send (ready, v)"  "accept v"
#   caller:  "take step 1"  "take step 2"  "take step 3"  "abandon"
#   BPR:     "retry (initial, v)"  "retry (echo, v)"  "retry (ready, v)"
#
# The caller applies: take step 1 -> send (echo, v) to all, have echoed;
# take step 2 -> send (ready, v) to all, have sent ready; take step 3 ->
# accept v, have accepted; abandon -> abandoned.  Step 0, send
# (initial, v) to all, is the caller's own send at Broadcast(v).  A
# retry re-sends the named message for INITIAL to every process not yet
# recorded as holding it, to all for echo, and for ready to the
# processes still owed it.  Three annotations of the caller's ride the
# messages BPR already re-sends, read off the instance when a message
# is framed: HOLDING, on (echo, v) and (ready, v), this process holds
# p's (initial, v) and what rode beside it; ACCEPTED, on (ready, v),
# this process has accepted; and RECEIVED, on (ready, v), set per
# recipient, this process holds that recipient's accept, a (ready, v)
# from it carrying ACCEPTED.  A re-send is retired by the evidence of
# the process it was sent to, about the thing sent, and by nothing
# else: no message of the figure says a process holds the initial or
# has accepted, and the others' echoes say nothing about either.  They
# are evidence a re-send reads; no row that decides a value reads one.
# An arriving (echo, v) or (ready, v) is recorded as the figure's from
# its sender and, carrying HOLDING, as its sender holding the initial;
# a (ready, v) carrying ACCEPTED as its sender having accepted; what a
# (ready, v)'s RECEIVED says is read by the row in
# bracha87Fig1Ready.dtc.

# ===== the paper's rows =====

# Step 1.  Wait until the receipt of one (initial, v) message, or more
# than (n+t)/2 (echo, v) messages, or t+1 (ready, v) messages, for some
# v.  Send (echo, v) to all the processes.
# A step is taken once: "have echoed" is the step having been taken.
@"send (echo, v)","have echoed","received (initial, v) from p","echo-count(v) > (n+t)/2","ready-count(v) >= t+1"
yes,no,yes,,
yes,no,no,yes,
yes,no,no,no,yes
no,no,no,no,no
no,yes,,,

# Step 2.  Wait until the receipt of more than (n+t)/2 (echo, v)
# messages or t+1 (ready, v) messages (including messages received in
# step 1), for some v.  Send (ready, v) to all the processes.
# "have echoed" is read after step 1 has been taken on this run.
@"echoed","have echoed","take step 1"
yes,yes,
yes,no,yes
no,no,no

@"send (ready, v)","echoed","have sent ready","echo-count(v) > (n+t)/2","ready-count(v) >= t+1"
yes,yes,no,yes,
yes,yes,no,no,yes
no,yes,no,no,no
no,yes,yes,,
no,no,,,

# Step 3.  Wait until the receipt of 2t+1 (ready, v) messages
# (including messages received in steps 1 and 2), for some v.
# Accept v.
# "have sent ready" is read after step 2 has been taken on this run.
@"sent ready","have sent ready","take step 2"
yes,yes,
yes,no,yes
no,no,no

@"accept v","sent ready","have accepted","ready-count(v) >= 2t+1"
yes,yes,no,yes
no,yes,no,no
no,yes,yes,
no,no,,

# ===== the caller's rows =====

# Step 1 is taken when the paper says send and, where p's (initial, v)
# is what enables it, this process holds the caller's something from p:
# a process echoes p's initial only with the caller's validation in
# hand, which is the one thing the library cannot do for it.  Enabled
# by the others' echoes or readies, step 1 is taken as printed: the
# first correct process to echo v did so on p's initial, with its
# caller's validation where its flag is set, since both counts need an
# earlier correct echo or ready, and the figure's "same value or none"
# (Theorem 1) rests on a process joining on their word.  A caller with
# nothing beside says so at Init, and the row never holds.
@"take step 1","send (echo, v)","hold the caller's something from p","echo-count(v) > (n+t)/2","ready-count(v) >= t+1"
yes,yes,yes,,
yes,yes,no,yes,
yes,yes,no,no,yes
no,yes,no,no,no
no,no,,,

# Steps 2 and 3 are taken as printed.
@"take step 2","send (ready, v)"
yes,yes
no,no

@"take step 3","accept v"
yes,yes
no,no

# Abandon, on a sweep, once the sweeps reach the abandon count.  Once.
@"abandon","event","sweeps since this process started","abandoned"
yes,sweep,>= abandon,no
no,sweep,>= abandon,yes
no,sweep,< abandon,
no,arrival,,

# ===== BPR's rows =====

# What this run leaves: still live, and whether p has accepted.
@"live","abandoned","abandon"
yes,no,no
no,no,yes
no,yes,

@"accepted","have accepted","take step 3"
yes,yes,
yes,no,yes
no,no,no

# The initiator re-sends (initial, v), to the processes not yet recorded
# as holding it, on every sweep while the instance is live and some
# process is not, accepted or not.  A process's echo on the others'
# echoes or readies is not its word that it holds the initial.
@"retry (initial, v)","event","am initiator","live","processes holding (initial, v)"
yes,sweep,yes,yes,not all
no,sweep,yes,yes,all
no,sweep,yes,no,
no,sweep,no,,
no,arrival,,,

# A sent echo is re-sent on every sweep until this process accepts
# (past accept, readies carry the rest: an accepted process holds 2t+1
# readies, t+1 of them correct and re-sent, and t+1 readies take any
# correct process holding the caller's something through steps 1 and 2
# without an echo).  Past that, p's re-send of its (initial, v) is p's
# word that it has not heard this process's HOLDING, and the echo that
# carries it is answered on that arrival (bracha87Fig1Ready.dtc).
@"retry (echo, v)","event","have echoed","accepted","live"
yes,sweep,yes,no,yes
no,sweep,yes,yes,
no,sweep,yes,no,no
no,sweep,no,,
no,arrival,,,

# A sent ready is re-sent on every sweep while the instance is live, to
# the processes still owed it.  No message of the figure says a process
# has accepted, so nothing of the figure retires it, and nothing local
# does: an accepted process still owes its ready to the processes below
# 2t+1.  An annotation riding the (ready, v) already re-sent does, per
# recipient: a process recorded as having accepted consumes no ready.
# A process that has accepted and lacks this process's accept says so
# on its own (ready, v), by the RECEIVED it does not carry, and is
# answered on that arrival (bracha87Fig1Ready.dtc), not on a sweep.
# Once every process is recorded, none is owed and the re-send retires
# -- quiescence, where abandon otherwise carries the tail.
# Under loss that is reachable, not guaranteed: a process that never
# carries ACCEPTED keeps the re-send to it until abandon.  That is what
# an abandoned or silent process looks like from here; the re-send is
# wire, and the process, if it is one of the t, is what t pays for.
@"retry (ready, v)","event","have sent ready","live","processes owed (ready, v)"
yes,sweep,yes,yes,some
no,sweep,yes,yes,none
no,sweep,yes,no,
no,sweep,no,,
no,arrival,,,
